---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="FinEquity"
  description="Market kinematics for the S&P 500 — the Kalman-filtered price level, its velocity and acceleration, and the jerk instability indicator, from the FinDynamics equity engine."
  eyebrow="Asset engine"
  heading="FinEquity — market kinematics"
  intro="Where the market is, how fast it is moving, whether that movement is strengthening or weakening, and which regime it is in. The daily record reaches back to 1927 and the monthly composite to 1871 — use the range control to see it. Everything on this page is read from the live API by your browser."
>
  <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="range-h">
    <div class="panel__head">
      <h2 id="range-h">Range</h2>
      <span class="panel__note">?range= &middot; every chart moves together</span>
    </div>
    <p class="prose">
      Each range is a separate request for that window, not a filter over one big
      payload &mdash; and the server downsamples to a point count a screen can actually show,
      preserving single-day crashes rather than sampling them away. Past twenty years the price
      axis is logarithmic: the index goes from 17 to over 7,000, and on a linear axis the first
      seventy years are a flat line on the floor.
    </p>
    <div id="equity-range" style="margin-top:0.85rem"></div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="k-h">
    <div class="panel__head">
      <h2 id="k-h">Kinematic state K(t)</h2>
      <span class="panel__note">/api/v1/state</span>
    </div>
    <p class="prose">
      The spec&rsquo;s Layer A: <em>what is the market doing?</em> Every value here is estimated
      from information available on its own date &mdash; the Kalman <strong>filtered</strong>
      estimate, never the smoother, because the smoother conditions on the whole sample and would
      put tomorrow&rsquo;s close into today&rsquo;s velocity.
    </p>
    <div id="equity-state" style="margin-top:0.85rem"></div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="regime-h">
    <div class="panel__head">
      <h2 id="regime-h">Regime posterior</h2>
      <span class="panel__note">/api/v1/regime</span>
    </div>
    <p class="prose">
      The five-state hidden Markov model of &sect;9, as a distribution rather than a label.
      Fitted on the daily record back to 1927 and applied to the published index; the states are
      mapped to names by a documented rule over their realized mean returns, and the mapping is
      asserted stable across refits. Probabilities are <strong>filtered</strong> &mdash; each date
      uses only what was known by then, never the smoothed pass that conditions on the whole sample.
    </p>
    <div id="equity-regime" style="margin-top:0.85rem"></div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="trans-h">
    <div class="panel__head">
      <h2 id="trans-h">Transition probability</h2>
      <span class="panel__note">AssetState.signals &middot; XGBoost + isotonic</span>
    </div>
    <p class="prose">
      Calibrated probability that the regime <em>enters</em> bear or crisis within each horizon,
      from a gradient-boosted classifier trained with purged cross-validation and a six-month
      embargo, then isotonically calibrated on held-out folds only. Read each against the tick
      marking its unconditional rate. These horizons are nested events, so the shorter can never
      exceed the longer.
    </p>
    <div id="equity-transitions" style="margin-top:0.85rem"></div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="rii-h">
    <div class="panel__head">
      <h2 id="rii-h">Regime instability</h2>
      <span class="panel__note">/api/v1/instability &middot; RII</span>
    </div>
    <p class="prose">
      &sect;3.2&rsquo;s composite, and the reason no fourth derivative is published anywhere on this
      site. Seven measurable components &mdash; the model&rsquo;s own posterior entropy and
      confidence deficit, jerk, volatility of volatility, the stock&ndash;bond correlation against
      its own baseline, credit velocity and liquidity stress &mdash; each scored 0&ndash;100 as a
      point-in-time expanding percentile. A component the information set cannot supply is
      <strong>named and dropped</strong>, and the weights renormalize over what remains; scoring it
      zero would read as &ldquo;maximally stable&rdquo;, which is the worst possible default for a
      broken data feed.
    </p>
    <div id="equity-instability" style="margin-top:0.85rem"></div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="crash-h">
    <div class="panel__head">
      <h2 id="crash-h">Crash risk &mdash; three factors</h2>
      <span class="panel__note">AssetState.signals &middot; &sect;4</span>
    </div>
    <p class="prose">
      Crash risk is never published as one number. It decomposes into the probability the regime
      turns, the probability a tail-sized shock arrives, and whether the system would transmit or
      absorb it &mdash; and the three call for different responses, which a product erases. The
      transition factor is derived here from the regime posterior and the transition matrix, not
      taken from the classifier: the classifier has no demonstrated out-of-sample skill, so
      transition probabilities are treated strictly as descriptive state shifts.
    </p>
    <div id="equity-crash" style="margin-top:0.85rem"></div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="forecast-h">
    <div class="panel__head">
      <h2 id="forecast-h">Forecast distribution</h2>
      <span class="panel__note">/api/v1/forecast &middot; Monte Carlo</span>
    </div>
    <p class="prose">
      Ten thousand paths per horizon, each starting from a regime drawn from today&rsquo;s
      posterior, stepping through the fitted transition matrix, with tail shocks drawn from the same
      generalized-Pareto fit the crash decomposition uses. The output is a set of quantiles and
      nothing else &mdash; there is no point estimate here because the table it is stored in has no
      column for one.
    </p>
    <div id="equity-forecast" style="margin-top:0.85rem"></div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="implication-h">
    <div class="panel__head">
      <h2 id="implication-h">Conditional implication</h2>
      <span class="panel__note">FINDYN_V1_SPEC.md &sect;12</span>
    </div>
    <p class="prose">
      The one place this system puts its numbers into a sentence &mdash; and the sentence is
      conditional on a constraint rather than addressed to a reader. &sect;0&rsquo;s second non-goal
      is &ldquo;no trading commands&rdquo;: no endpoint in this API returns an action, and this
      block is assembled in your browser from the values shown in the panels above, so there is
      nothing behind it you cannot check.
    </p>
    <div id="equity-implication" style="margin-top:0.85rem"></div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="price-h">
    <div class="panel__head">
      <h2 id="price-h">Filtered price against the close</h2>
      <span class="panel__note">/api/v1/assets/equity/history?metric=price_close &amp; price_filtered</span>
    </div>
    <p class="prose">
      The raw index close and the filtered level of the local-linear-trend model. The filter is
      what makes the derivatives below meaningful: differencing a raw index twice is mostly
      microstructure, because differencing amplifies noise roughly in proportion to the order.
      Both series are in index points here; the model works in logs. The filter runs over the
      whole daily record back to 1927-12-30, so both lines are drawn wherever the market has
      one &mdash; there is no range at which the model stops and the raw data carries on alone.
    </p>
    <div id="equity-price" style="margin-top:0.85rem"></div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="kin-h">
    <div class="panel__head">
      <h2 id="kin-h">Velocity and acceleration</h2>
      <span class="panel__note">/api/v1/assets/equity/history?metric=velocity &amp; acceleration</span>
    </div>
    <p class="prose">
      Velocity is the filter&rsquo;s trend state, annualized &mdash; the drift the model currently
      believes in, not the last two closes. Acceleration is its rate of change, per year squared.
      A positive velocity with negative acceleration is a rally losing its slope, which is the
      distinction this panel exists to make visible.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      Both are state estimates over the full daily record, so the 1929&ndash;32 collapse and the
      1990s expansion are on the same axis as this quarter. The first year of each is not
      published: a Kalman filter starts from a diffuse prior and the slopes it produces on the
      way down describe that prior rather than the market.
    </p>
    <div id="equity-kinematics" style="margin-top:0.85rem"></div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="jerk-h">
    <div class="panel__head">
      <h2 id="jerk-h">Jerk &mdash; trend instability</h2>
      <span class="panel__note">/api/v1/assets/equity/history?metric=jerk_z</span>
    </div>
    <p class="prose">
      The third derivative is deliberately demoted. Financial series are noisy and a raw third
      derivative is almost entirely noise, so it is published <strong>only</strong> as a z-score
      against the series&rsquo; own expanding history, read through a threshold: calm, elevated
      beyond two standard deviations, extreme beyond three. It says the trend is becoming
      unstable. It does not say which way.
    </p>
    <div id="equity-jerk" style="margin-top:0.85rem"></div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="forces-h">
    <div class="panel__head">
      <h2 id="forces-h">Force state F(t)</h2>
      <span class="panel__note">/api/v1/forces</span>
    </div>
    <p class="prose">
      The spec&rsquo;s Layer B inputs: <em>why is it doing this?</em> Kept strictly apart from the
      kinematics above, because price derivatives and economic drivers answer different questions
      and mixing them produces a number that answers neither. Each score is a point-in-time
      expanding-window percentile on one axis &mdash; 100 is maximally risk-supportive &mdash; and
      each carries the breakdown that produced it.
    </p>
    <div id="equity-forces" style="margin-top:0.85rem"></div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="provenance-h">
    <div class="panel__head">
      <h2 id="provenance-h">Where the numbers come from</h2>
      <span class="panel__note">model_version &middot; series provenance</span>
    </div>
    <div id="equity-provenance"></div>
  </section>
</Base>

<script>
  import '../scripts/equity';
</script>
